<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Heshen</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1750">1750</a>–<a href="/years/1799">1799</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Hešen, Shanbao, Zhizhai</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Heshen was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchus">Manchu</a> official of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing dynasty</a>, of the Niohuru clan and the Plain Red Banner, who rose from imperial bodyguard to become the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qianlong-emperor">Qianlong Emperor</a>'s favourite, holding several top posts at once. His son married the emperor's favourite daughter in 1790, and by Qianlong's abdication in 1796 he had become practically a regent. He extorted gifts, filled posts with his own men and stole public and military funds, and has been described as the most corrupt official in Chinese history. After Qianlong died in 1799 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiaqing-emperor">Jiaqing Emperor</a> charged him with 20 crimes, confiscated a fortune estimated at 60 to 80 million taels and ordered him to hang himself.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/heshen.jpg" alt="Heshen" width="330" height="613" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hesen.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Salaries grew less and less adequate, and officials came to rely on “squeeze”, irregular payments; in Qianlong’s last years his favourite <b>Heshen</b> became one of the most corrupt officials of the dynasty.</blockquote><a href="/societies/qing-china">Social Hierarchy of Qing China</a></li></ul></section>
